-- gpsr-distance-sales-non-eu-sellers
UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyC$# EU General Product Safety Regulation: distance-sale listing duties for non-EU sellers

The EU General Product Safety Regulation, Regulation (EU) 2023/988, applies since 13 December 2024 and modernises the EU product safety framework for the online age. Its Article 19 sets specific information duties for distance sales: any online offer targeting EU consumers must display defined manufacturer, responsible-person, product and safety information before the consumer buys. If you sell into the EU from outside the EU, this is the rule that governs your product listings.

**Short answer:** Since 13 December 2024, the EU General Product Safety Regulation requires every online offer targeting EU consumers to display the manufacturer's identity and contacts, the EU responsible person's details where the manufacturer is outside the EU, product identification including images and batch or serial information, and all required warnings in the language of the member state of sale.

## Key facts

- The GPSR (Regulation (EU) 2023/988) applies since 13 December 2024 and is the EU's general product safety law for the online age.
- It applies to most consumer products not covered by sector-specific EU harmonisation legislation, and it imposes a general safety requirement: only safe products may be placed on the EU market.
- Article 19 requires distance-sale offers targeting EU consumers to display four information items before the consumer is bound: manufacturer identification, EU responsible-person details, product identification, and warnings and safety information.
- An offer is considered to target EU consumers when it is directed at them, including through a non-EU webshop.
- Any product covered by the GPSR placed on the EU market needs an EU-based economic operator: the manufacturer (if EU-established), the importer, an authorised representative, or a fulfilment service provider.
- The Regulation modernises market surveillance for online trade, sets accident-reporting duties, and contains detailed rules for economic operators, online marketplaces and fulfilment service providers.
- The responsible-person duty does not replace the manufacturer's own safety obligations; it adds an EU contact point for authorities and consumers.

## What the GPSR is

The GPSR is the EU's general product safety law. It applies to most consumer products not covered by sector-specific EU harmonisation legislation, and it imposes a general safety requirement: only safe products may be placed on the EU market. It modernises market surveillance for online trade, sets accident-reporting duties, and gives detailed rules for economic operators, including online marketplaces and fulfilment service providers.

The regulation's structure matters for sellers. Where sector-specific EU harmonisation law applies (for example toys, machinery, radio equipment), that law applies first and the GPSR fills the gaps. Where no sector law applies, which covers a large share of general consumer goods such as furniture, household articles, sports equipment and many lifestyle products, the GPSR is the primary safety regime. In both cases, the distance-sale information duties in Article 19 apply to online offers, because they attach to the offer, not only to the product's sector classification.

Beyond listings, the GPSR brings a full safety-management framework: a general safety requirement assessed against criteria the regulation sets out (including the product's characteristics, its effect on other products, and its presentation and labelling), traceability duties, technical documentation demonstrating the safety assessment, and accident-reporting duties when a product presents a risk. Sellers who treat the GPSR as "just a labelling rule" miss most of it.

## Article 19: the distance-sale information duties

An offer for distance sale is considered to target EU consumers when the offer is directed at them, including through a non-EU webshop. Before the consumer is bound, the offer must make available:

1. **Manufacturer identification:** name, registered trade name or trademark, plus postal and electronic address.
2. **EU responsible person:** where the manufacturer is not established in the EU, the name, postal and electronic address of the responsible person established in the EU.
3. **Product identification:** information allowing identification of the product, including a picture, the type, and any batch or serial number.
4. **Warnings and safety information:** any warning or safety information that must accompany the product, in a language easily understood by consumers in the member state of sale.

For example, a US electronics brand selling through its own site to German consumers must show its EU responsible person's details on each product page, and a UK toy seller listing on a marketplace must ensure the listing carries the required warnings in the local language.

The practical reading of "before the consumer is bound" is that the information must be visible in the offer itself, not buried in terms and conditions or supplied after checkout. That means product-page templates, marketplace listing fields and data feeds all need dedicated slots for the four items. For sellers with large catalogues, the work is a data project: every SKU needs manufacturer details, responsible-person details, product identifiers and warning text per language, flowing into every sales channel. A single missing element on a single listing is a non-compliance, so the template approach (mandatory fields that block publishing when empty) beats manual checking.

Language is the item sellers most often get wrong. Warnings and safety information must appear in a language easily understood by consumers in the member state of sale, which in practice means the official language(s) of each country targeted. Machine-translating safety warnings without review is a false economy: an inaccurate warning is worse than a missing one from a liability perspective. Build a translation workflow with qualified review for safety content, and version-control the warning text per market so updates propagate.

## Who needs an EU responsible person

Any product covered by the GPSR that is placed on the EU market needs an EU-based economic operator: the manufacturer (if EU-established), the importer, an authorised representative, or a fulfilment service provider. Non-EU manufacturers cannot place products on the EU market without one of these in place. However, the responsible-person duty does not replace the manufacturer's own safety obligations; it adds an EU contact point for authorities and consumers.

Choosing the structure is a commercial and legal decision. An EU importer that takes title to the goods naturally fills the role and carries the importer's verification duties. A direct-to-consumer brand with no EU importer typically appoints an authorised representative by written mandate, which should define the products covered, the tasks delegated and the information flows (the representative needs access to the technical documentation). A brand holding stock in an EU fulfilment warehouse with no other EU operator may find the fulfilment service provider falling into the role by default, which is a backstop to avoid by design rather than a structure to rely on.

Whatever the structure, the responsible person's details must actually appear in the listings, and the person must actually be able to perform: receive authority inquiries, produce documentation, and coordinate corrective actions. A name on a listing with no operational backing behind it fails at the first enforcement contact. Verify the arrangement works end to end before launch, including a test of the documentation-production chain.

## Safety assessment, traceability and technical documentation

The listing duties sit on top of a substantive safety regime. The GPSR's general safety requirement means the product must be safe under normal and reasonably foreseeable conditions of use, assessed against the criteria the regulation sets out. Manufacturers must draw up technical documentation demonstrating the safety assessment, keep it available, and ensure traceability: the product must be identifiable (type, batch or serial number) and the economic operators traceable.

For sellers, this means the listing information must match the physical product and the documentation. The batch or serial number shown in the offer should correspond to the traceability marking on the product; the warnings in the listing should match the warnings supplied with the product; and the manufacturer details should match the technical file. Inconsistencies between listing, product and file are exactly what market surveillance checks look for, because they signal a compliance process that exists on paper only.

Accident reporting is the duty sellers least expect. Economic operators must notify authorities where they become aware that a product they placed on the market presents a risk, through the mechanisms the regulation provides, and must maintain internal processes for collecting and assessing safety signals. Set up an accident-reporting channel and an internal procedure before launch: a monitored contact point, a defined escalation path to the responsible person, and a record of decisions. Online marketplaces have parallel duties to handle safety signals on their platforms, so align your procedure with each marketplace's reporting tools.

## Marketplaces, fulfilment and enforcement

Online marketplaces carry their own GPSR duties, including cooperation with market surveillance authorities, handling of dangerous-product notifications, and traceability of traders on their platforms. In practice this means marketplaces increasingly require sellers to provide responsible-person details, product identifiers and safety documentation as a condition of listing, and they can remove listings that lack them. Brief marketplace account teams early: marketplace listings must carry the same Article 19 information as a brand's own webshop, and the data feed must keep it current.

Fulfilment service providers are in scope as economic operators with defined duties, and as noted they can become the responsible person by default. If you use EU fulfilment, clarify in the contract who holds the technical documentation, who receives authority inquiries, and who bears the cost of corrective actions. Do not assume the warehouse contract covers this; most standard 3PL terms do not.

Enforcement runs through market surveillance authorities in each member state, coordinated at EU level, with dangerous products notified in Safety Gate. Authorities test products bought online, check listings for the Article 19 information, and can order withdrawals and recalls. The GPSR's online-age design means enforcement reaches the listing as easily as the product: a non-compliant offer can be acted against even before a physical sample is tested.

## Comparison: EU, UK, US

| Market | Responsible-person concept | Pre-purchase listing information |
|---|---|---|
| EU | EU-based responsible person required under the GPSR for covered products | Article 19: manufacturer, responsible person, product identification and warnings before the consumer is bound |
| United Kingdom | UK-based responsible economic operator needed under the UK product safety regime | UK-specific information duties; EU responsible-person details do not satisfy UK rules |
| United States | No general pre-market responsible-person requirement for consumer products under the CPSC framework | Labelling duties (warnings, tracking labels for children's products) rather than a pre-purchase responsible-person disclosure |

Listings and operator structures must be built separately for each market. A single global product page cannot satisfy all three regimes; build market-specific templates.

## Compliance checklist

- [ ] Appoint an EU-based responsible person (importer, authorised representative or fulfilment service provider) for every product line.
- [ ] Verify the responsible person can actually perform: documentation access, authority cooperation, corrective-action capacity.
- [ ] Audit every online listing targeting the EU for the four Article 19 information items, in every sales channel.
- [ ] Ensure warnings and safety information appear in the language of each member state of sale, with qualified translation review.
- [ ] Add product identification (type, batch/serial, images) to each offer, consistent with the physical product marking.
- [ ] Keep technical documentation demonstrating the product's safety assessment, matched to the listing content.
- [ ] Set up an accident-reporting channel and internal procedure, aligned with marketplace reporting tools.
- [ ] Brief marketplace account teams: marketplace listings must carry the same information; keep data feeds current.

## What this means for businesses

For businesses, Article 19 turns every product page into a compliance surface. The practical work is unglamorous: data feeds, listing templates and translation workflows must carry the responsible-person details and warnings for each market. Non-EU sellers should treat the EU responsible person as a prerequisite, not an afterthought, because marketplaces and payment providers increasingly ask for it. Budget for multilingual safety information and for keeping technical documentation current across product variants. However, the same data discipline that satisfies Article 19 also streamlines marketplace onboarding, reduces delisting risk and shortens authority inquiries, so it pays back as operational resilience. Build the listing template once, with mandatory compliance fields, and every new SKU inherits the structure.

**Frequently asked questions:**

## Does Article 19 apply to my non-EU webshop?

If the offer targets EU consumers, yes. An offer is considered to target EU consumers when it is directed at them, including through a non-EU webshop, for example by offering EU shipping, EU languages or EU currencies.

## Can my EU importer be the responsible person?

Yes, an importer established in the EU can fulfil the role, and commonly does. The importer then also carries the importer's verification and post-market duties.

## Do second-hand products fall under the GPSR?

The Regulation has specific provisions for second-hand, repaired and reconditioned products; check the text for your case rather than assuming either way.

## What about products already covered by sector legislation?

Sector-specific EU harmonisation law applies first; the GPSR fills the gaps. The Article 19 distance-sale duties still attach to the online offer.

## What language must warnings be in?

A language easily understood by consumers in the member state of sale, in practice the official language(s) of each targeted country. Have safety translations reviewed by qualified translators.

## What happens if a listing misses the responsible-person details?

The listing is non-compliant. Marketplaces can remove it, and market surveillance authorities can act against the offer. Treat the four Article 19 items as mandatory listing fields.

## Assumptions and limitations

- This guide focuses on Article 19 distance-sale duties; the GPSR also contains safety assessment, traceability and accident-reporting rules covered in our general GPSR guide.
- Language requirements follow the member state of sale; this guide does not list them per country.
- Digital products and services have specific considerations outside this guide's scope.

## Official sources

- [Regulation (EU) 2023/988 on EUR-Lex (English)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/oj/eng)
- [Regulation (EU) 2023/988, CELEX full text](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32023R0988)

